**Read:** [The Physics of Water Treatment Design — AguaClara](../sources/Physics-of-Water-Treatment-Design-AguaClara.lingua#r_1V2KMP22J5KMQF65K8Q7ZXXBAR), the full carbonate reactions, buffering and pH chapter; [Industrial Water Treatment Operation and Maintenance — UFC 3-230-13](../sources/Industrial-Water-Treatment-UFC-3-230-13.lingua#r_41KNKBCA6GZWQ6VAJGAPG2HJ3R), makeup-water chemistry and interpretation of scaling conditions.

pH, alkalinity and hardness are different quantities. pH concerns hydrogen-ion activity; alkalinity is an acid-neutralizing capacity defined by a chemical convention and measurement; hardness commonly accounts for multivalent cations, especially calcium and magnesium. Similar pH values can occur with very different buffering.

For a carbonate-dominated dilute model, a common alkalinity expression is [HCO₃⁻] + 2[CO₃²⁻] + [OH⁻] − [H⁺], in equivalents per volume. Additional acid–base systems contribute in real waters. Using rounded equivalent mass, 2 meq/L is about 100 mg/L as CaCO₃; this does not mean 100 mg/L of dissolved calcium carbonate was measured.

State whether the water is open to gas exchange and whether minerals can react. Carbon dioxide exchange changes carbonate speciation and pH without simply acting like addition of a strong acid. Construct an acid/base and carbonate account for a treatment step, then compare it with a titration and the conditions under which the sample was held.

